# Black Is the Colour: traditional ballad, after Cecil Sharp (1916). The traditional
# tune, not John Jacob Niles' melody (still in copyright).
{title: Black Is the Colour}
{composer: Traditional (Scotland/Appalachia)}
{key: Am}
{tempo: 96}
{time: 3/4}
{x_level: 2}
{x_strum: D - D - D -}

{start_of_grid label="Verse"}
| Am . . | Am . . | G . . | Am . . |
| C . . | C . . | G . . | Am . . |
| Am . . | Am . . | Em . . | Am . . |
| C . . | C . . | G . . | Am . . |
{end_of_grid}

{comment: Sing the traditional tune (Sharp, 1916), not John Jacob Niles' later composed melody.}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
But [Am]black is the colour of [G]my true love's [Am]hair,
His [C]face is something [G]wondrous [Am]fair;
The [Am]purest eyes and the [Em]neatest [Am]hands,
I [C]love the grass where[G]on he [Am]stands.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
I [Am]love my love and [G]well he [Am]knows,
I [C]love the grass where[G]on he [Am]goes;
If [Am]he on earth no [Em]more I [Am]see,
My [C]life will quickly [G]fade a[Am]way.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
The [Am]winter's passed and the [G]leaves are [Am]green,
The [C]time is passed that [G]we have [Am]seen,
But [Am]still I hope the [Em]time will [Am]come
When [C]you and I shall [G]be as [Am]one.
{end_of_verse}
{x_end: Am}
